To reduce repetition, we can generalize our code to use a loop. Take a simple task such as outputting the first 1000 positive integers: we could write 1000 cout statements, but that would be painful. Or we could use a loop.

Without a loop (partial):
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  cout << 1 << endl;
  cout << 2 << endl;
  cout << 3 << endl;
  // hundreds more cout statements
  cout << 999 << endl;
  cout << 1000 << endl;
  return 0;
}

With a loop:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  for(int i=1; i <= 1000; i+=1 )
  {
    cout << i << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}

Suppose we are writing a program for a simple turn-based guessing game where the user must guess a secret number. If they guess incorrectly, what should we do?

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  if(guess == secretNum) {
    cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  }
  else {
    /* What should we do here? */
  }
  return 0;
}

What if they guess wrong a second time? What should we do?

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  if(guess == secretNum) {
    cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  }
  else {
    cin >> guess;
    if(guess == secretNum) {
      cout << "You got it!" << endl;
    }
    else {
      /* What should we do here? */
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

We can never write enough if statements because someone might always use one more turn than we have if statements. But we see there is a repetitive structure in this code. Let's use a loop.

Example (three nested guesses):
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  if(guess == secretNum) {
    cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  }
  else {
    cin >> guess;
    if(guess == secretNum) {
      cout << "You got it!" << endl;
    }
    else {
      cin >> guess;
      if(guess == secretNum) {
        cout << "You got it!" << endl;
      }
      else {
        /* What should we do here? */
      }
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

There are 2 (really 2.5) kinds of loops: while (do..while) and for loops. We'll now cover how they work.

while example:
int i = 1;
while (i <= 1000)
{
  // repetitive task
  cout << i << endl;
  i++; // update
}
// following statements

for example:
int i;
for (i = 1; i <= 1000; i++)
{
  cout << i << endl;
}
// following statements

There is a variant of the while loop which is the do..while loop, covered later. 4 parts: Initialization, Condition, Body, Update.

Loops involve writing a task to be repeated. Regardless of that task, there must be 4 parts to make a loop work:
- Initialization: Initialization of the variable(s) that will control how many iterations the loop will execute. Executed only once!
- Condition: Condition to decide whether to repeat the task or stop the loop.
- Body: Code to repeat for each iteration.
- Update: Modify the variable(s) related to the condition (without the update, the condition could be TRUE forever, leading to an 'infinite loop').

for(int i=1; i <= 1000; i+=1 )
{
  cout << i << endl;
}
// Code after

int i=1;
while(i <= 1000) {
  cout << i << endl;
  i++;
}
// Code After

A while loop is essentially a repeating 'if' statement.

if version:
// initialization
if (condition)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
}
// following statements

while version:
// initialization
while (condition)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
  // update statement
} // go to top, eval cond1 again
// following statements

The major change between if and while is that after executing the block, while loops back to re-evaluate the condition, rather than proceeding to the following statements.

A while loop is essentially a repeating 'if' statement.

initialization
while (condition1)
{
  // Body: if condition1 is true
  // Update statement
} // go to top, eval cond1 again
// following statements
// only gets here when cond1 is false

The numbered trace (steps 1-9, evaluating condition as True, True, False across iterations) walks through the flowchart boxes: Initialization (e.g. i=1) -> Condition (e.g. i <= 1000) -> Loop task (e.g. cout << i << endl) -> Update Statement (e.g. i += 1) -> back to Condition -> ... -> Code after the loop once condition is False.

What do we put in a while or for loop body? ANYTHING! This includes expressions and variable assignment, function calls, if..else statements, and even other loops.

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
  int guess;
  int secretNum = /* some code */
  cin >> guess;
  while(guess != secretNum)
  {
    cout << "Enter guess: " << endl;
    cin >> guess;
  }
  cout << "You got it!" << endl;
  return 0;
}

