while loops have a sibling known as do..while loops. do..while loops start with keyword do, followed by the body of code to be executed repeatedly in brackets { }, and end with while condition and semicolon (;). do..while loops will execute the body at least once.

Example:
int main()
{
  int x, y, val;
  bool quit;
  // a while loop
  while( x < val )
  {
    /* body of code */
  }
  // a do..while loop
  do
  {
    /* body of code */
  } while( x < val );
  return 0;
}

do..while loops check the condition after executing at least once and repeat if the condition is true.

while syntax:
while (condition)
{
  // executed if condition1 is true
} // go to top, eval cond1 again
// following statements
// only gets here when cond1 is false

do..while syntax:
do
{
  // executed at least once
} while (condition); // go to 'do' (top) if cond1 evals to true
// following statements
// only gets here when cond1 is false

do..while loops check the condition after executing at least once and repeat if the condition is true.

do
{
  // executed at least once
} while (condition); // go to 'do' (top) if cond1 evals to true
// following statements
// only gets here when cond1 is false

The numbered trace (steps 1-7, evaluating condition as True, True, False) walks through the flowchart: Body executes first (step 1), then Condition is checked (step 2), looping back to the body if true, continuing until the condition is false and control passes to Following statements.

